aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r3, to: Suppose "possibly, in efficient causes one goes on to infinity" is true. Is S4, "Now in efficient causes it is not possible to go on to infinity, because in all efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate cause, and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate cause, whether the intermediate cause be several, or only one.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 3 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 has "infinite → ¬first ∧ ¬ultimate ∧ ¬intermediate" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2 has "infinite → ¬first" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; they differ when only "in efficient causes one goes on to infinity", "there is an intermediate efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate effect" are true, which the second allows; aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3 has "◇infinite → ¬first" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S7 is read aquinas-second-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.2 with no modality; aquinas-second-way.logic.r3.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r3, to: Suppose "in efficient causes one goes on to infinity", "there is an intermediate efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate effect" are true and "there is a first efficient cause" and "possibly, in efficient causes one goes on to infinity" are false. Is S7, "But if in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity, there will be no first efficient cause, neither will there be an ultimate effect, nor any intermediate efficient causes; all of which is plainly false.", true then?
